## Waiting on a decision

- The base snapshot, and whether workspaces share the mirror's objects by
  alternates. Measured: mirror 13 GB, the single snapshot's `.git`
  14 GB, and only the 12.5 GB base pack hardlinked between them (`%n links` ==
  2). Every pack the snapshot fetched after its clone has one link, so each
  `wk sync` writes the same objects into the mirror and again into the snapshot,
  and a workspace's own fetch writes them a third time into its overlay upper.

  Yes, it is possible, via `git clone --reference`. Refs must NOT be shared -- a
  ref is mutable and per-workspace, so a shared one means a workspace moving its
  branch moves the mirror's. Objects are what gets shared, and an alternate beats
  a hardlink: one 40-byte file, no inode per pack, survives a repack, and sees
  every newly fetched object the moment the mirror has it.

  Two things to agree before building it:

  1. The mirror becomes load-bearing forever -- `git gc --prune` on it would
     corrupt every workspace borrowing from it. `gc.auto 0` is already set;
     `wk gc` must refuse to prune it while anything references it. That is one
     new invariant in exchange for the ones it retires (half-published
     snapshots, `base_verify`, completion markers, `current_base`, the SNAP
     column, "N newer snapshots").
  2. The mirror must be mounted at the **same path** inside a container as on the
     host, because an alternates path has to resolve on both sides and the
     relative offsets differ (`/mirror/WebKit.git` vs `$WK_STORE/git/WebKit.git`).
     Every existing workspace has to be remade.

