- rpi5: **root-caused and fixed in code; the board still needs one interactive
  run.** Both halves were one bug. The helper *is* installed and byte-identical,
  but `wk sudo setup`'s `/etc/sudoers.d/zz-<user>-passwd` says `PASSWD: ALL`,
  `ALL` matches the helper too, and `wk-quiesce` sorted *before* it -- so the
  carve-out was dead (`sudo -n wk-quiesce-priv status` -> 1), `wk quiesce` and
  `wk session` prompted for a password in the daily path, and `./setup` reported
  the helper missing on every run and never converged. That is what "does not
  persist" was.

  Fixed: the rules are named `zzz-wk-quiesce` / `zzz-wk-card` so they are the
  last match; `./setup` and `wk doctor` now distinguish "not installed" from
  "installed but out-ranked"; `wk sudo setup` checks it has not out-ranked them.

  Owed: run `./setup --stage quiesce` on rpi5 **interactively** -- there is no
  non-interactive sudo there, which is the point of the drop-in, so it cannot be
  repaired remotely. Every other machine that has run `wk sudo require`/`setup`
  needs the same run; this Mac still has the old `/etc/sudoers.d/wk-quiesce`.

  Not the cause, checked and ruled out: `./setup` is otherwise idempotent on
  rpi5 (two consecutive runs, no changes, empty diff), and everything it writes
  is on the NVMe ext4 root -- no tmpfs, no overlay, and no reboot in 3d21h.

- `wk sync --machine` on a macOS host does the fleet and tooling half and then
  hands the store half over by naming the command to run. One verb that finishes
  the job on a Mac needs the dispatcher to split a single invocation across the
  host and the VM, which is a change to `wk`'s forwarding rather than to
  `cmd/sync`.
